		<section class="timeline">
			<h2>Timeline of Irish History from 1800 - 2000</h2>
			<a class="source" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_Irish_history">Information and text
				sourced from wikipedia</a>

			<!-- div used to group all the events together-->
			<div class="timeline-events">
				<h3 id="1800s">1800s</h3>
				<article>
					<h4>1801 — Acts of Union</h4>
					<p>The Kingdom of Ireland unites with Great Britain, forming the United Kingdom of Great
						Britain
						and Ireland, coming into effect on 1 January 1801.</p>
				</article>
				<h3 id="1820s">1820s</h3>

				<article>
					<h4>1829 — Catholic Emancipation</h4>
					<p>The Roman Catholic Relief Act is passed on 24 March 1829, allowing Catholics to
						sit in
						the UK
						Parliament for the first time.</p>
				</article>
				<h3 id="1840s">1840s</h3>
				<article>
					<img style="float: right; height: 7vw;" src="images/famine.jpg"
						alt="A painting of a scene from the Irish Famine">
					<h4>1845–1849 — The Great Famine</h4>
					<p>A potato blight destroys two-thirds of Ireland's staple crop, leading to an estimated
						one
						million deaths and the emigration of a further million people, transforming Irish
						society
						and the global Irish diaspora forever.</p>
				</article>
				<h3 id="1910s">1910s</h3>
				<article>
					<img style="float: right; height: 5vw;" src="images/GPO.jpg"
						alt="A photograph of the GPO during the Easter Rising">
					<h4>1916 — The Easter Rising</h4>
					<p>On 24 April 1916, the Irish Republican Brotherhood seizes key government buildings in
						Dublin
						and issues the Proclamation of the Irish Republic. The Rising lasts until 29 April.
					</p>
				</article>

				<article>
					<img style="float: right; height: 10vw;"
						src="images/Irish_Declaration_of_Independence.jpg"
						alt="An image of the Irish Declaration Of Independence">
					<h4>1919 — The First Dáil & War of Independence</h4>

					<p>On 21 January 1919, the First Dáil of the Irish Republic meets and issues a Declaration
						of
						Independence. On the same day, IRA volunteers kill two members of the Royal Irish
						Constabulary in the first act of the War of Independence.</p>
				</article>
				<h3 id="1920s">1920s</h3>
				<article>
					<h4>1921 — The Anglo-Irish Treaty</h4>
					<p>Signed on 6 December 1921, the Anglo-Irish Treaty ends the War of Independence and
						establishes the Irish Free State. Northern Ireland is separately established on 3
						May of
						the same year.</p>
				</article>

				<article>
					<h4>1922–1923 — The Irish Civil War</h4>
					<p>On 28 June 1922, Michael Collins orders the bombardment of Anti-Treaty forces at the
						Four
						Courts, beginning the Civil War. It ends on 24 May 1923 when IRA Chief of Staff
						Frank
						Aiken orders volunteers to dump arms.</p>
				</article>
				<h3 id="1930s">1930s</h3>
				<article>
					<h4>1937 — A New Constitution</h4>
					<p>On 29 December 1937, the Constitution of Ireland comes into force, replacing the Irish
						Free
						State with a new state officially named Éire, or Ireland in the English language.
					</p>
					<h5>Main Points</h5>
					<ul>
						<li>Ireland was to be fully independent</li>
						<li>Free speach</li>
						<li>Freedom of religion</li>
						<li>Equality before the law</li>
					</ul>
				</article>
				<h3 id="1940s">1940s</h3>
				<article>
					<h4>1949 — The Republic of Ireland</h4>
					<p>On 18 April 1949, the Republic of Ireland Act abolishes the statutory functions of the
						British monarch in relation to Ireland, formally declaring the Republic.</p>
				</article>
				<h3 id="1960s">1960s</h3>
				<article>
					<img style="float: right; height: 6vw;" src="images/troubles.jpg"
						alt="A image of a scene from the Irish Troubles">
					<h4>1969 — The Troubles Begin</h4>
					<p>In August 1969, British troops are deployed on the streets of Northern Ireland, marking
						the
						beginning of The Troubles — three decades of conflict that would shape the island's
						modern
						history.</p>
					<h5>Most affected areas:</h5>
					<ol>
						<li>Belfast</li>
						<li>County Armagh</li>
						<li>County Tyrone</li>
						<li>County Down</li>
						<li>Derry City</li>
					</ol>
				</article>
				<h3 id="1990s">1990s</h3>
				<article>
					<h4>1998 — The Belfast Agreement</h4>
					<p>Signed in April 1998, the Belfast Agreement (also known as the Good Friday Agreement)
						brings
						an end to the Troubles, establishing a power-sharing Northern Ireland Assembly and a
						new
						framework for relations across the island.</p>
				</article>

				<article>
					<img style="float: right; height: 5vw;" src="images/euro.png" alt="An image of ">
					<h4>1999 — Adopting the Euro</h4>
					<p>On 1 January 1999, Ireland relinquishes the Irish pound and adopts the Euro, joining
						the
						single European currency alongside ten other EU member states.</p>
				</article>
			</div>

